WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.409
Hey guys, welcome back to the channel.

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:09.929
My name is Dami and I'm an architect in Vancouver.
So today we're gonna talk about five skills that

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:14.100
you can start developing now, to prepare for architecture school.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.020
[Acoustic Music]

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.200
You can start practicing these now

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.340
and they're gonna help build the foundation for your journey, not just in architecture

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.410
school, but on your path to becoming an architect.
So when you start architecture

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.249
school, there's gonna be so much information
thrown at you, that having these basic tools

00:00:34.249 --> 00:00:39.519
under your belt will help you absorb more
of that information, help you get a leg up

00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:46.170
on your peers, and they might also help you avoid those
all-nighters. These are things you

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:52.089
can practice little by little every day until
you start school. Also, by the end of the

00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:57.469
video, I will also teach you a little bonus
trick that you can do with a q tip that has

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:01.329
saved me a lot of time for me when I was in school.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:07.500
The first couple items I will cover all has
to do with your communication skills, which

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:14.500
is kind of essential to your success in architecture.
Your education is gonna be composed of: architectural

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:21.500
history, theory, building science, structures,
sustainability, urban planning, and finally,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:28.009
your design studio. So the design studio is where
you take the culmination of all the things

00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:35.659
that you've learned, and create your own design out of it.
This project is gonna be your baby, a very

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:43.000
needy baby. It's gonna need feeding and nurturing,
it's gonna be constantly on your mind when

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.679
you're in the shower or when you go to bed,
and you

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:53.549
will be responsible for turning it into something
amazing, and when it sucks, everyone is gonna

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:55.810
judge you and you will fail.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:00.229
But it's also the best and most fun part of
architecture school.

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:05.570
Anyways, as you go through the design process,
you're constantly presenting to your peers,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:11.590
your instructors, whether it's during desk
crits or during your final presentation.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:18.599
The most important thing to remember 
is: show, don't tell. You don't want to just

00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:25.120
talk about your ideas, you actually want to show them,
with sketches, drawings, or renders.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:34.430
So the first skill i want you to practice
every single day, is sketching.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:34.930
[Acoustic Music]

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:35.430
We now use digital

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.760
modelling and computer renderings but sketching
is still so important for you to get your

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.540
ideas out quickly and effectively.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:48.799
If you're not good at sketching, that's totally
fine. That's what practice is for. This is

00:02:48.799 --> 00:02:53.370
something i learned in life drawing classes,
it's that when you're just trying to learn,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.790
don't go and try drawing the entire body.
just try focusing on a small portion of it,

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:03.720
like the feet. so when you're at home, find
an object that you find interesting and

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.750
try to draw a very small portion of it.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:12.129
You really don't need to
be a perfect drawer or get all the perfect

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:17.910
little details. I think what's most important
is just being able to express your ideas with

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.950
your sketches, quickly.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:26.580
So personally, I like to figure out the spatial
relationships and the basic massing on paper,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:33.090
and then I move into 3d modelling fairly quickly,
because it's, for me, the fastest way to iterate

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.239
through a bunch of different massing options.

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:42.840
A crucial thing to always keep in mind is,
you think by doing. A lot

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:48.450
of students miss this. You'll get a project
brief, and they spend way too much time trying

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:54.739
to figure out the initial concept, or come up
with the initial parti. and when it's time

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:59.950
for the desk crit, they only have a few scribbles. The concept is something you develop

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:07.360
over the span of your project, and it evolves
as you go through the design process.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:14.060
So when you're thinking or designing, just sketch
out a bunch of different iterations, try to overlay

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.640
them on trace, by the process

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:24.130
of creating those things, you'll be able to see what works
and what doesn't work and you can eliminate from there.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:32.160
The modmin Youtube channel has some really great sketching
tips, he also has a tutorial on rapid sketching,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.950
so you can check that out.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:40.320
Also, try to practice drawing some plans.
You can start off my copying or tracing the

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:48.840
plans of some of the masters and just try to think about
why they shaped the building a certain way,

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:57.550
what that does to the spaces and the movement
or light conditions in those spaces, and what

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:01.010
that means to the overall concept of the building.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:05.770
I would love to see your progress, so if you're
drawing or sketching, follow me on Instagram

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:06.930
and tag me.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:11.840
If you're struggling in the beginning in the beginning of school, don't even worry. I was a late

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:20.400
bloomer. I was terrible in school until second
year. And if you're like me, you will have that one

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:27.220
project that will just ignite that fire in you.
For me, it was a hydrotherapy pool. That project

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.610
really made me fall in
love with architecture and the whole process

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:37.009
behind it. So yeah, just be patient, you will find
that project too.

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:46.280
The second thing you can start working on
right now, is all the digital tools.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:46.780
[Acoustic Music]

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:52.789
when i was in school, we didn't have a dedicated
course for learning all the programs, and

00:05:52.789 --> 00:05:57.520
you were supposed to learn them on your own, so knowing how to use them right into

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.830
school will give you a huge advantage.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:08.340
The most common ones are AutoCAD, SketchUp,
Rhino, and Revit. I would say Sketchup is

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:15.050
probably the easiest for you to learn, and you can probably learn it in just

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:21.919
a week or two. with sketchup you can create
whatever you want. Simple to more complex

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:29.490
geometries, and you can render them afterwards.
I'll put together a playlist of Sketchup tutorials

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:30.490
that you can watch.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:36.900
Then I would say try to learn AutoCAD, that's
the software where you draw your floorplans,

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:44.100
elevations and sections. it's an industry
standard, so if you're looking to get a summer

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:51.349
job, this and Revit will be very helpful to have.
It's a bit more complicated, but learning

00:06:51.349 --> 00:06:56.110
all the functions and shortcuts, they're gonna will help
you further down the line.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:02.979
The third program is Rhino. Rhino is kind
of like Sketchup but you can do more things

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:08.150
with it. If you only have time to learn one
of them, I would say

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:15.840
Rhino. You can just create more complex geometries,
and I know that some architects say that the

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:23.740
software is just an extension of your pencil,
but i really think that the infinite amount

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:31.750
of 3d modelling possibilities in rhino has
really helped me in the creative process, and it's helped

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:37.590
me design geometries that probably would
have taken me a very long time to figure out by hand

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:43.460
or on AutoCAD. For example, this project,

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:52.419
I wanted to create a torquing motion throughout
the building, and I just started with a few

00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:59.250
sketch models, sketched out a few plans, and
just moved straight into Rhino, because I

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:05.190
could quickly iterate through a bunch of options
and geometries, and I knew it was gonna take me

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:07.600
forever to figure it out on paper.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:14.851
There's actually tons of really great rhino
tutorials on youtube, you can just search Rhino

00:08:14.851 --> 00:08:20.460
Tutorials, and their channel will take you from
step 1.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:26.979
And then there's Revit. Revit is an industry
standard. it's

00:08:26.979 --> 00:08:32.780
what you call a BIM software. So basically to
create a building you would draw an actual

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:40.750
wall in the category of a wall, rather than
drawing in two lines or a geometry that represents

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:47.110
a wall, as you would do in AutoCAD or Rhino.
Then you would put in a roof, in the category

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:54.700
of a roof. For example if you were to model
these two exact same things in Rhino, Rhino would just treat

00:08:54.700 --> 00:09:03.300
this as a geometry, but Revit understands
that this is a wall, and that this is a roof, a window. Revit's

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:08.910
an industry standard so it's a really great skill to
have if you want to work at an arch firm over

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:09.920
the summer.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.650
But there's some pros and cons.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:21.170
The pro, is that once you create the walls
or roofs, the 3d model is basically just created for you,

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:33.290
or visa versa. So once you create the 3d model, then you already have
the 2d drawings, like the plans, sections, and elevations

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:40.490
so this obviously feels like a no brainer.
Why would you go through Rhino, then Autocad

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:45.580
to prepare the drawings when you can just
do it in Revit? Right?

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:52.040
So here's the con. Revit is a great tool to know
when you start working in the industry and

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:59.130
definitely it creates a lot of efficiencies in
the process but I think the danger of using Revit

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:05.560
in school is that, it can kind of limit
your creativity. So for example, the default

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:13.370
wall in revit is a 2 by 4 stud wall going
straight up. In architecture school, you want

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:19.080
to challenge the conventional idea of what
a wall is, what a building looks like. so you

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:27.100
have to go through extra steps to design something
that has a slightly more complex geometry.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:34.320
I think when the default wall or roof looks
like the industry standard, then your inclination

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:43.570
is to design boxy, very standard looking buildings.
And I have seen projects change throughout

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:48.460
the years, once Revit was introduced in schools.
So it's definitely something to think about.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:53.150
The workflow that I have used when I was doing my Masters, is I would do

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:58.170
all my designs and modelling in Rhino, and
I recreated the model in Revit to create the

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:03.700
floorplans. Coz I still like the way Revit creates the orthographic drawings

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:09.640
So Udemy has really good affordable Revit course
for beginners, I took it and I have a couple

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:15.940
friends took it. So I'll put the link below.
If you use my link, you won't pay a penny

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:20.790
more but I will get a little tip from the
course.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:26.500
Also, if you can teach yourself all the Adobe suites,
like InDesign, Photoshop Illustrator, that's

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:32.920
also a bonus, because you will definitely be using all
of them in school. There's tons of tutorials

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:36.630
you can find on Youtube or other online learning
platforms.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:37.130
And skill number three

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:37.630
[Music]

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:47.440
Understanding the basics of perspectives
and composition helps a lot when you're doing

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.580
renderings, which you will be producing a
lot of

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.100
[Music]

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:58.220
or for photographing your models.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.160
[Music]

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:06.750
There's some general rules that you can follow,
like keeping all your lines perfectly horizontal

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:13.620
or perfectly vertical. I do post-processing
on all of my images, and it can also be a

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:17.760
fun way for you to learn programs
like Photoshop or Lightroom.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:27.080
I mentioned briefly earlier, your courses
will contain your core architectural courses,

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.000
and your design course.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.880
[Music]

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:36.860
One of those architectural
courses is architectural history, where you

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:43.180
learn about the different architectural styles,
and the culture, religion, or politics that

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:44.420
shaped that architecture.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:50.880
Learning about the architectural past it will teach you how to think about architecture

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:54.380
and talk about architecture in a more meaningful

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.600
and intelligible way.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:01.650
This stuff is really interesting when you
actually spend the time to learn about it,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:08.590
but when you're in school, and you have all
these other things that you have to do,

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:14.510
learning about the medieval churches might not
be the first thing on your mind. Like I remember

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:18.810
having a kind of hard time focusing on this particular course.

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:24.320
So i think this is something that you could
try to learn when you have a little

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:25.620
bit more time now.

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:31.680
So I've created a playlist for you on my channel
where you can learn about these different styles

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:38.220
in architecture. Some of these videos can
be a little bit drab, so if you find any of them  are kind of boring

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.780
just skip through them.

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:45.480
This is a book that you will probably be a
required reading for school, so you might

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:51.620
as well just get it now, it covers all the
different styles in a kind of chronological

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:57.580
order. It talks about cultural influences
that shaped the architecture. But it is kind of

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:04.900
dense, maybe reading one section a day
could help you get a little bit more into it.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:08.160
[Music]

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:15.520
So in the first year of architecture school,
I was so invested in my studio project that

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:22.880
i spent all my time into it, and a lot of my other core courses, they kinda suffered.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.880
and I did lot of all nighters.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:29.400
I mean, I can't stop
you from doing them. If you gotta do it, you

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:30.580
gotta do it.

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:36.390
It can be kinda tricky to avoid those all
nighters because you might have a new idea like

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:43.840
five days before your deadline, or your prof
tells you to change something, and you're

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:48.610
like Agh F#$% I have to do it all over again.

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:58.050
And so what I would say about this, is that. Try to do a lot of work in the front end. Don't wait til a couple

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:06.440
weeks in to the deadline to make some big
decisions in your design. Do as many iterations

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:14.770
as possible. And just throw out all your bad ideas.
The more developed your idea is, it will be

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:20.810
easier to sell it to your professors during
your crits, and you're gonna be more convinced

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:28.290
of your idea as well. I think a lot of students fall into the trap of trying to find the best idea,

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:33.280
but what's more interesting is the process
and the story behind the idea.

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:39.390
During my thesis, we would have the hand in
deadline for your work, where you submit all

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:47.550
your materials, and between that time and the
presentation, that was about a week, and during that time, you

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:53.060
could figure out your presentation, and how
to tell the story behind your project. I really

00:15:53.060 --> 00:16:02.130
liked that approach, because everyone had very polished presentations.

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:02.630
ultimately, it doesn't matter how amazing your project is

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:06.390
if you can't convince your clients that it's
an amazing idea.

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:14.330
So all that is to say. Start learning time
management. Learn how to manage yourself.

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:18.630
Keep this constantly in the back of your mind
as you're going through your daily life and

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:20.840
as you go through architecture school.

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:24.600
This is also connected to mental health.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:33.500
mental health is the core of everything.
School is just a stepping stone for you to get to the end

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:41.730
goal, which is to become a professional who is going to contribute to the betterment of

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:48.830
society. You can't have a positive influence
on other people or become a mentor to them if you're

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:56.220
not healthy here. Mental health is just like
physical health. It's something you have to

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:01.710
practice every day. Otherwise, you will get
burnt out. I have seen so many architects

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.000
quit and complain about their work

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.930
So start getting in the habit of taking a

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:13.991
little bit of time to yourself, preferably
doing something that's off the screen. Maybe

00:17:13.991 --> 00:17:23.959
it's a little bit of meditation, quick cardio, or yoga. Take some time to think about what you're doing,

00:17:23.959 --> 00:17:26.730
instead of just going through the motions.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:32.670
This reflection can actually help you with
your workflow as well. I think stepping back

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:39.020
to think about your struggles and inefficiencies can be a really good thing to incorporate into your

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:40.170
daily lifestyle.

